 {:db/id "q-94304/ma-source-field/df8205593f0afbefdc35a91f0c6dc237305334e55acac0d9c55b838b51583b0e" :answer-field/id #uuid "039eefc7-4800-59f4-a9af-2eb142d7c4e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-94304/ma-source-field/df8205593f0afbefdc35a91f0c6dc237305334e55acac0d9c55b838b51583b0e/eaac7a2a44d3917ee28944910c9a9c1afaab9b1d631707a5f84fc5b80149328c" "q-94304/ma-source-field/df8205593f0afbefdc35a91f0c6dc237305334e55acac0d9c55b838b51583b0e/0cde7213d1e1ba3c98aefd0b0b3cd2806e45d0ed74e5b8251efd6e11b0b57d5b" "q-94304/ma-source-field/df8205593f0afbefdc35a91f0c6dc237305334e55acac0d9c55b838b51583b0e/5229d35e6ea963bad83d7a13a9082603ad8608b16ddc393c8758eed7acf155fe" "q-94304/ma-source-field/df8205593f0afbefdc35a91f0c6dc237305334e55acac0d9c55b838b51583b0e/bdb020ee4b4ad82d771e73ed8614590494deb5a5ab3119e61abaabb8986935c7" "q-94304/ma-source-field/df8205593f0afbefdc35a91f0c6dc237305334e55acac0d9c55b838b51583b0e/a47a7cb242c9c03d85735ac66563c40c4f15c828bdc43334468badfc8f708afa"] :answer-field/correct "q-94304/ma-source-field/df8205593f0afbefdc35a91f0c6dc237305334e55acac0d9c55b838b51583b0e/eaac7a2a44d3917ee28944910c9a9c1afaab9b1d631707a5f84fc5b80149328c" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-94304" :question/id #uuid "1f3b61bf-2d9c-5a4f-b827-01f0e0d4bec0" :question/math-academy-id "q-94304" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in centimeters, of a particle oscillating with forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+49y=-24\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Given that the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, find the position of the particle after $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "The general solution to the equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ represents simple harmonic motion and can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=49\\,⟹\\,\\omega =7.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡7t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡7t.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t).$\n\nThe external force is $-24\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,\\beta $ are constants to be determined.\n\nSubstituting ${y}_{p}$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{2},\\beta =0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡7t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡7t-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSince the particle is initially at the origin with zero velocity, the initial conditions are $y(0)=0$ and ${y}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\nSolving with these conditions for $A$ and $B$ gives\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2},\\,B=0.\n$$\n\nSo our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡7t-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ seconds, we substitute into the equation $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{2})-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-94304/ma-source-field/df8205593f0afbefdc35a91f0c6dc237305334e55acac0d9c55b838b51583b0e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
