 {:db/id "q-9461" :question/id #uuid "c118445f-8af4-5515-9f4b-7c6040808075" :question/math-academy-id "q-9461" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse ${x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}+6x+6y=3$  intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}+6x+6y & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2}+3\\cdot {0}^{2}+6x+6\\cdot 0 & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x & =3 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}-9 & =3 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =3+9 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =12 \\\\ x+3 & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ x & =-3\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at $(-3-2\\sqrt{3},0)$ and $(-3+2\\sqrt{3},0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-9461/ma-source-field/2fa2777102f1167240e81a4e3d059cc8f260f106c458469e02619e2ec5136009"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
