 {:db/id [:question/id #uuid "f68108cf-9e6a-5782-b0ed-5257eaff8ba9"] :question/problem "Determine the points where the ellipse $3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6\\sqrt{3}x-6y+6=0$ intercepts the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $y$-intercepts, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6\\sqrt{3}x-6y+6 & =0 \\\\ 3(0)^{2}+{y}^{2}-6\\sqrt{3}(0)-6y+6 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-6y+6 & =0 \\\\ (y-3)^{2}-9+6 & =0 \\\\ (y-3)^{2} & =3 \\\\ y-3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{3} \\\\ y & =3\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,3-\\sqrt{3})$ and $(0,3+\\sqrt{3}).$\n\n![](images/de/de18c7e39dd73d5f72fd32b34b97b65cf0011e6da44b36a1d4b3d62df8852a9a.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-9472/ma-source-field/07195d37d2113865aa5e520c8e66de2123cc1595818dd49683802b502fdbc763"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
