 {:db/id "q-94754" :question/id #uuid "f3490611-7455-53f0-adf9-bb4a980b5cf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-94754" :question/problem "Consider the set ${{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)}$ of polynomials from $R[t],$ where\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=2+{t}^{2},\\,{p}_{2}(t)=1+t+{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the set is linearly dependent, find the value of $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}$ from a non-zero solution of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}\\cdot {p}_{2}(t)=0.$" :question/worked-solution "If the set is linearly dependent, then there exist ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ not both zero such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)=0.\n$$\n\nOtherwise, if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0,$ the set is linearly independent.\n\nSubstituting the given expressions for ${p}_{1}(t)$ and ${p}_{2}(t)$ into the above equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(2+{t}^{2})+{x}_{2}(1+t+{t}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ (2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2})+({x}_{2})t+({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}){t}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation is true for every $t$ if and only if \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ Substituting this into the third equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=0.$ \n\nTherefore, ${{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly independent." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-94754/ma-source-field/a3d3e5eff56a7bb8d5ffc23088fb62baab9854f954c3e7f44bb15e0e3001b12b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
