 {:db/id "q-94989" :question/id #uuid "d9d89d1b-ccd0-5adb-bbd0-8964c61e5651" :question/math-academy-id "q-94989" :question/problem "Which curve is defined by the equation $4{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=10?$" :question/worked-solution "Consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=4{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.$ To determine the curve defined by the given equation, we reduce $Q(x)$ to its canonical (diagonal) form. \n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of $Q(x),$ which is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =5,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our quadratic form can be reduced to $5{y}_{1}^{2}.$ \n\nIn the new variables, the given curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}_{1}^{2} & =10 \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents the two parallel lines ${y}_{1}=\\sqrt{2}$ and ${y}_{1}=-\\sqrt{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-94989/ma-source-field/28ad2c792aa3667a65dd9dfa77adc30e25ee417c42abf35bfd66a6eb48a1ee8d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
