 {:db/id "q-95015" :question/id #uuid "2b7fa421-8274-5a15-90a7-f07c79709eb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-95015" :question/problem "Which curve is defined by the equation $-5{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}=12?$" :question/worked-solution "Consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=-5{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}.$ To determine the curve defined by the given equation, we reduce $Q(x)$ to its canonical (diagonal) form.\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of $Q(x),$ which is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-5-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -24 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =4,\\,-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our quadratic form can be reduced to $4{y}_{1}^{2}-6{y}_{2}^{2}.$ \n\nIn the new variables, the curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}_{1}^{2}-6{y}_{2}^{2} & =12 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\sqrt{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-95015/ma-source-field/da25fe516305b9b196967711a8f57115bc51a297aa5cccb40f833a63cf7bb3a7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
 {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5d6ac06-40b4-5da0-bd26-860b35826292"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-95015"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}
