 {:db/id "q-95805/ma-source-field/873d49e0efc27b5b8efcd70809bd9596f9a1fc0c923d2e678f078976a26b747c" :answer-field/id #uuid "03775e48-d8c6-5bdb-abb2-c177de91a6fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-95805/ma-source-field/873d49e0efc27b5b8efcd70809bd9596f9a1fc0c923d2e678f078976a26b747c/4e51f8a3f183f7977bd81ad046e4f85a754015f569247134789d2f70d585d67c" "q-95805/ma-source-field/873d49e0efc27b5b8efcd70809bd9596f9a1fc0c923d2e678f078976a26b747c/1eadd010c5094e2d33eaf76f4322873b0a1d7af1be08272ab40f2ea8b7063c2e" "q-95805/ma-source-field/873d49e0efc27b5b8efcd70809bd9596f9a1fc0c923d2e678f078976a26b747c/370c356d324c7aae6b112b643fdfb2c2a7bdaadb6fe4ba16e8453a535e3f1bce" "q-95805/ma-source-field/873d49e0efc27b5b8efcd70809bd9596f9a1fc0c923d2e678f078976a26b747c/2c8dd2717b4b539cf3fd995c4237442e1fcd1094d45c8172682cf61c8e4e46de" "q-95805/ma-source-field/873d49e0efc27b5b8efcd70809bd9596f9a1fc0c923d2e678f078976a26b747c/b63bf283a78bf3276dfbd3678bb21affdeea1cd25e362531a83ef978fcac20f0"] :answer-field/correct "q-95805/ma-source-field/873d49e0efc27b5b8efcd70809bd9596f9a1fc0c923d2e678f078976a26b747c/370c356d324c7aae6b112b643fdfb2c2a7bdaadb6fe4ba16e8453a535e3f1bce" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-95805" :question/id #uuid "0fc392a3-f500-5b88-9a2e-df6cdd3a73e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-95805" :question/problem "Find the principal axes of the curve $9{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-6{x}_{2}^{2}=5.$" :question/worked-solution "First, consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=9{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-6{x}_{2}^{2}.$ The matrix of the form is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is symmetric. The principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=5\n$$\n\nare the vectors that form an orthonormal eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\nBy solving the characteristic equation $|A-\\lambda I|=0,$ we obtain\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=10\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-7.$\n\nLet's now find the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-10I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-10I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{17},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-7)I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{aligned}]+7[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}16 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-1I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{17},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-95805/ma-source-field/873d49e0efc27b5b8efcd70809bd9596f9a1fc0c923d2e678f078976a26b747c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
