 {:db/id "q-95912/ma-source-field/f407424d2ac6a724ef68e713b1accc3d2286887f190899bb46a7ec411679ebee" :answer-field/id #uuid "ca27652a-1905-5111-863a-956b813d6f8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-95912/ma-source-field/f407424d2ac6a724ef68e713b1accc3d2286887f190899bb46a7ec411679ebee/475fc29e2e1e49d083d5a9a125c1a8ee3bfc88b838cb208c4d516fe07ba6043a" "q-95912/ma-source-field/f407424d2ac6a724ef68e713b1accc3d2286887f190899bb46a7ec411679ebee/272bcd94e93f26c4c329514d719eab9de57fd613faa1b6bf71abd3cf4fb7f956" "q-95912/ma-source-field/f407424d2ac6a724ef68e713b1accc3d2286887f190899bb46a7ec411679ebee/226d563d1b34ce1b2436cc9e3f02e4f09f9127ae05f213a1a5b823e357710d20" "q-95912/ma-source-field/f407424d2ac6a724ef68e713b1accc3d2286887f190899bb46a7ec411679ebee/9b707b967ab2113c7e78acca7f7a210fd84dab5a76129fa8c63ac09e92191398" "q-95912/ma-source-field/f407424d2ac6a724ef68e713b1accc3d2286887f190899bb46a7ec411679ebee/71b9e8c825dba94b0c3e6b543300bd85c529d783d2b189e734f92721536a4110"] :answer-field/correct "q-95912/ma-source-field/f407424d2ac6a724ef68e713b1accc3d2286887f190899bb46a7ec411679ebee/272bcd94e93f26c4c329514d719eab9de57fd613faa1b6bf71abd3cf4fb7f956" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-95912" :question/id #uuid "069d96cb-6a82-58f7-9653-ba67b55165b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-95912" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 13\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=4$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=1.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=16,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-16I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 13\\end{aligned}]-16[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-12 & -6 \\\\ -6 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-16I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 13\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-95912/ma-source-field/f407424d2ac6a724ef68e713b1accc3d2286887f190899bb46a7ec411679ebee"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
