 {:db/id "q-95963/ma-source-field/98935f3727edf71982486696f1a5f9a78f4c5ea06fe0bfaac15d90b067de83a1" :answer-field/id #uuid "e3edb909-2e0f-52b6-afa6-af45414c142a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-95963/ma-source-field/98935f3727edf71982486696f1a5f9a78f4c5ea06fe0bfaac15d90b067de83a1/f150b3e928712aa440b18d16b3050fd47d0336dc13773f706593c1dd9d1e05ac" "q-95963/ma-source-field/98935f3727edf71982486696f1a5f9a78f4c5ea06fe0bfaac15d90b067de83a1/b63c7a5850d5e8ccd08a6586de429cc1319921a23f62bf8f3b1b2ea4e74b8463" "q-95963/ma-source-field/98935f3727edf71982486696f1a5f9a78f4c5ea06fe0bfaac15d90b067de83a1/6dd529f5fba666ab87a387244946a43dfdae1da8a144ac71f2105bad73c2657a" "q-95963/ma-source-field/98935f3727edf71982486696f1a5f9a78f4c5ea06fe0bfaac15d90b067de83a1/80d73a7efb10547a4527f5555ace0be8fbc101bedc513ecee17d1710239550b3" "q-95963/ma-source-field/98935f3727edf71982486696f1a5f9a78f4c5ea06fe0bfaac15d90b067de83a1/2820b5378c9aa4f9543e749e8b453011a785aad1cc126fb30b926d7d1e11cf6f"] :answer-field/correct "q-95963/ma-source-field/98935f3727edf71982486696f1a5f9a78f4c5ea06fe0bfaac15d90b067de83a1/2820b5378c9aa4f9543e749e8b453011a785aad1cc126fb30b926d7d1e11cf6f" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-95963" :question/id #uuid "e6e7a4f8-5474-5944-8860-1961f0b9c75e" :question/math-academy-id "q-95963" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: Notice that since ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is diagonal, the singular values of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\sigma }_{1}={\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{10}.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$): Seeking non-zero solutions of $({A}^{T}\\,A-10I)v=0,$ we obtain the unit eigenvectors \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U,$ $Σ,$ and $V:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{10} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-95963/ma-source-field/98935f3727edf71982486696f1a5f9a78f4c5ea06fe0bfaac15d90b067de83a1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
