 {:db/id "q-95981/ma-source-field/bd73a16deb37eb2a300d11da32fb0d9cfea868a5cf150f4f7980bd50885d6bcb" :answer-field/id #uuid "c3303f09-b1cf-58c2-80d3-c7c5d852de3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-95981/ma-source-field/bd73a16deb37eb2a300d11da32fb0d9cfea868a5cf150f4f7980bd50885d6bcb/c91a13c2943427314f8bcc190414e62696ce0e8e2051e7a3a4c8e5b865ab4ce5" "q-95981/ma-source-field/bd73a16deb37eb2a300d11da32fb0d9cfea868a5cf150f4f7980bd50885d6bcb/4016326c4f4ab9ab89644c45c046ed7434c44cf20e51f01fadd4b6d7beadfc80" "q-95981/ma-source-field/bd73a16deb37eb2a300d11da32fb0d9cfea868a5cf150f4f7980bd50885d6bcb/b5981268baee43b5639fba99e4e04c94d99c8daa8e557ff1d7fade5b185e5812" "q-95981/ma-source-field/bd73a16deb37eb2a300d11da32fb0d9cfea868a5cf150f4f7980bd50885d6bcb/e62eb306f03e554813d9794674cfee9db81f8f530f7f343833169dfda4dd64da" "q-95981/ma-source-field/bd73a16deb37eb2a300d11da32fb0d9cfea868a5cf150f4f7980bd50885d6bcb/6be0966fa73787ee9ebf5cb82e72575a99353c18cec9f3579a4f4d4700314f27"] :answer-field/correct "q-95981/ma-source-field/bd73a16deb37eb2a300d11da32fb0d9cfea868a5cf150f4f7980bd50885d6bcb/c91a13c2943427314f8bcc190414e62696ce0e8e2051e7a3a4c8e5b865ab4ce5" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-95981" :question/id #uuid "c0349f24-f5b9-5625-bd79-9fb74dd2ec96" :question/math-academy-id "q-95981" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{10} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: Notice that from the matrix $Σ,$ we can deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{10}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$ without solving the characteristic equation.\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=10,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-10I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-10I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-0I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A{A}^{T}$): Since the first singular value is not zero, and we know the corresponding singular right vector, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, we can't use the same formula to find the second column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,‖{u}_{2}‖=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get a nonzero solution $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Normalizing this vector, we finally obtain the  second column of $U:$ \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-95981/ma-source-field/bd73a16deb37eb2a300d11da32fb0d9cfea868a5cf150f4f7980bd50885d6bcb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
