 {:db/id "q-95998" :question/id #uuid "ca23a2a3-705b-526c-8692-0652a1e980bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-95998" :question/problem "What type of quadratic curve is defined by the following equation?\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{17}{4}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the squares for the variables ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} & =-\\frac{17}{4} \\\\ ({x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1})+({x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{2}) & =-\\frac{17}{4} \\\\ ({x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2})+({x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}) & =-\\frac{17}{4} \\\\ ({x}_{1}+2)^{2}-{2}^{2}+{({x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =-\\frac{17}{4} \\\\ ({x}_{1}+2)^{2}-4+{({x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4} & =-\\frac{17}{4} \\\\ ({x}_{1}+2)^{2}+{({x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nA sum of squares is equal to zero if and only if each summand is zero, so the given quadratic equation represents the single point $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2})$ in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-95998/ma-source-field/9e7576d2b462811355e7c8ad31d08e77ea2cc604d996e9c8887ae3753adc5eb0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
