 {:db/id "q-96351/ma-source-field/731f9de323cc16759cafd568a8d2fef8268dc84ac2c70d3761f48c3652a6c49a" :answer-field/id #uuid "325d739f-4267-5a10-9368-e2ffaa069a1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-96351/ma-source-field/731f9de323cc16759cafd568a8d2fef8268dc84ac2c70d3761f48c3652a6c49a/e691b883d27b3cc51f75e0eacba73bef0214d9bcdb4f81fd7840979f7d8a38d7" "q-96351/ma-source-field/731f9de323cc16759cafd568a8d2fef8268dc84ac2c70d3761f48c3652a6c49a/f76203c3fa30719d4dd59b79a5e53690fe60c92a547fdd7d0541a63d201ca302" "q-96351/ma-source-field/731f9de323cc16759cafd568a8d2fef8268dc84ac2c70d3761f48c3652a6c49a/7dfe831b8f1268c272813f5ea2e048854a472f7720075580d2dbe7add17a2e5f" "q-96351/ma-source-field/731f9de323cc16759cafd568a8d2fef8268dc84ac2c70d3761f48c3652a6c49a/655bea3074b36ca0cdc0e17ff80980a27ff8dbbb3d31edef3d2bfe750b08eb05" "q-96351/ma-source-field/731f9de323cc16759cafd568a8d2fef8268dc84ac2c70d3761f48c3652a6c49a/c1e360d48b6679a1b54c8a7a41a45f17954cc01d7c7555dab41d50a5d1cb48c7"] :answer-field/correct "q-96351/ma-source-field/731f9de323cc16759cafd568a8d2fef8268dc84ac2c70d3761f48c3652a6c49a/c1e360d48b6679a1b54c8a7a41a45f17954cc01d7c7555dab41d50a5d1cb48c7" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id [:question/id #uuid "b0a7a3b6-717f-5db5-9c33-ba5621dfa097"] :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: Notice that from the matrix $Σ,$ we can deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{6}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$ without solving the characteristic equation.\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-6I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-0I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A{A}^{T}$): Since the first singular value is not zero, and we know the corresponding singular right vector, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, we can't use the same formula to find the second column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,‖{u}_{2}‖=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}}{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get a nonzero solution $[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Normalizing this vector, we finally obtain the  second column of $U:$ \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-96351/ma-source-field/731f9de323cc16759cafd568a8d2fef8268dc84ac2c70d3761f48c3652a6c49a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
