 {:db/id "q-96980/ma-source-field/49776c9f802e3330b25d69fa908aa1737aa8dd65ba61d5fd0afa8d05af95ccfc" :answer-field/id #uuid "de7aee45-41d5-558c-b353-ad3a0dc35b5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-96980/ma-source-field/49776c9f802e3330b25d69fa908aa1737aa8dd65ba61d5fd0afa8d05af95ccfc/4e418a2ce12b62e7fd02a3766c156886d4e5d5a01d3beff6cfb8d00a8fa2d462" "q-96980/ma-source-field/49776c9f802e3330b25d69fa908aa1737aa8dd65ba61d5fd0afa8d05af95ccfc/9dad8400dd5215ba3b7133753fee5b128c2815dda38283fdc5d8280805d351b1" "q-96980/ma-source-field/49776c9f802e3330b25d69fa908aa1737aa8dd65ba61d5fd0afa8d05af95ccfc/d49873f7353a488f76dfbd8416d7d11b5c8cc1ce564859549fca4df28cbfc0cc" "q-96980/ma-source-field/49776c9f802e3330b25d69fa908aa1737aa8dd65ba61d5fd0afa8d05af95ccfc/48ae09762c6944172cc31b5c6dc0b6ff426466a4a0039a1059d78cec94085125" "q-96980/ma-source-field/49776c9f802e3330b25d69fa908aa1737aa8dd65ba61d5fd0afa8d05af95ccfc/fde7c7e8c5c0e1280c4619c1897573fc8396a25055092e595f0052889d677717"] :answer-field/correct "q-96980/ma-source-field/49776c9f802e3330b25d69fa908aa1737aa8dd65ba61d5fd0afa8d05af95ccfc/fde7c7e8c5c0e1280c4619c1897573fc8396a25055092e595f0052889d677717" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-96980" :question/id #uuid "98f4b936-523f-5fef-91ea-f05cd3f88ed0" :question/math-academy-id "q-96980" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf the matrix $A$ above is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the matrix $A$ is triangular, its eigenvalues are the entries of the main diagonal. Therefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ (simple root) and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$ (double root).\n\nLet's now find the dimension of the eigenspace corresponding to each eigenvalue.\n\n- First, we consider the simple root ${\\lambda }_{1}=1.$ Since the root is simple, the dimension of the eigenspace ${V}_{1}$ corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{1})=1.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the double root ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Let's find the dimension of the eigenspace ${V}_{-1}$ corresponding to this eigenvalue. To do this, we row-reduce the matrix $A-(-1)I:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). This implies that we will have $3-2=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. So, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{-1})=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ is *not* diagonalizable, since \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({V}_{1})+\\text{dim}({V}_{-1})=1+1=2\\ne 3.\\,\\times \n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-96980/ma-source-field/49776c9f802e3330b25d69fa908aa1737aa8dd65ba61d5fd0afa8d05af95ccfc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
